course: world
lesson: 59
title: Inventions, computers and the internet
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An invention is a new thing or a new way of doing something that solves a problem. Inventors build on earlier ideas: the bicycle needed the wheel, and the car needed both the wheel and the engine.
explain: Some inventions changed how people share knowledge. The printing press made books cheap, so many more people could learn to read; the telephone and the radio carried voices far away almost instantly.
explain: A computer is a machine that follows instructions called a program. Inside, it stores everything — letters, pictures, sounds — as numbers, made in the end of just two signals, 0 and 1.
explain: The internet is a huge network that joins computers all over the world. A message is cut into many small packets, each labelled with the address it must reach. The packets travel through cables, through glass fibres as flashes of light, and through the air as radio waves, passing from one router to the next.
explain: At the end, the receiving computer puts the packets back together in the right order. A website is stored on a computer called a server, and your device asks the server for it. Messages can be copied and passed on, so think before you share anything online.
fact: An invention is a new thing or method that solves a problem.
fact: Most inventions are built on earlier inventions and ideas.
fact: The printing press made books cheaper and helped knowledge spread.
fact: A computer follows a list of instructions called a program.
fact: A computer stores letters, pictures and sounds as numbers made of 0s and 1s.
fact: The internet is a network that joins computers around the world.
fact: Messages on the internet are split into small packets, each with an address.
fact: Packets travel through metal cables, glass fibres and radio waves.
fact: Fibre-optic cables carry information as flashes of light.
fact: A router is a machine that passes packets on towards their address; if one path is broken, routers send the packets another way.
fact: Wi-Fi and mobile phones send information through the air as radio waves.
fact: A website is stored on a computer called a server.
fact: Anything posted online can be copied and kept by others, so personal details should stay private.
exercise: What is an invention? | A new thing that solves a problem | Anything found lying about in nature | Any old tool that has been repaired | A story about a famous person's life
exercise: Why could the car not be invented before the wheel? | Inventions build on earlier inventions | Cars were banned in ancient times | Nobody wanted to travel long ago | Wheels were invented long after engines
exercise: How did the printing press change the world? | Books became cheaper and more people could read | It made all books disappear | It made writing by hand illegal | It made books more expensive and rare
exercise: What is a computer program? | A list of instructions | The screen of a computer | The electricity a computer uses | A kind of computer cable
exercise: How does a computer store a photo? | As numbers made of 0s and 1s | As a tiny printed picture inside | As drops of coloured ink | As a small mirror image
exercise: What is the internet? | A network that joins computers all over the world | One giant computer in one country | A program inside every phone | A cloud in the sky that keeps everyone's data safe
exercise: What happens to an email before it travels across the internet? | It is cut into small packets with an address | It is printed out and posted in a letter box | It is turned into sound | It is sent whole as one big piece
exercise: How does information travel through a fibre-optic cable? | As flashes of light | As a stream of water | As sound echoing inside | As tiny pieces of paper
exercise: How does a phone send information without a cable? | Using radio waves through the air | Using sound you cannot hear | Using a magnet pointing at the tower | Using light from the screen
exercise: Where is a website stored? | On a computer called a server | Inside the screen of your phone | In the clouds of the sky | In the cable under the street
exercise: What does a router do? | Passes packets on their way | Prints messages out on paper | Makes electricity for the network | Stores all the world's websites
exercise: Why should you not share your home address with strangers online? | Things posted online can be copied and kept by anyone | The internet deletes every address at once, so it is safe | Addresses make computers slow | It is impossible to type an address
exercise: Which invention first let people speak to each other across long distances by wire? | The telephone | The printing press | The bicycle | The light bulb
test: A video from a friend in another country arrives on your tablet. What really happened? | It travelled as many small packets that were put back together | It flew through space in one piece | It was copied by hand and posted | It was already inside your tablet
test: A ship's anchor damages an undersea cable. What happens to internet messages that used it? | Routers send the packets along other paths | All of the internet in the world stops for ever | The messages turn into sound waves | The messages sink to the seabed
test: Which is true about how computers keep information? | Words, pictures and music are all stored as numbers | Computers keep tiny printed copies of every page inside | Only words can be stored in a computer | Computers remember things the way people dream
test: Why can a fibre-optic cable carry so much information so fast? | It uses flashes of light | It is filled with very fast water | It is much thicker than a copper wire | It uses sound, which is faster than light
test: A girl posts a photo and then deletes it an hour later. Is it surely gone? | No, someone may already have copied it | Yes, pressing delete removes every single copy | Yes, photos vanish after an hour | No, but only because the server froze
test: Your laptop shows a page from a library's website. Where was the page kept before you asked for it? | On the library's server computer | On a piece of paper in the library | Inside your laptop all along | In the Wi-Fi radio waves
test: Why did the printing press help science grow faster? | Scientists could share their ideas with many readers quickly | It made experiments happen by themselves | It made paper stronger than metal | It stopped people arguing about ideas
test: A program tells a robot "move forward, turn left, move forward". What happens if one step is missing? | The robot does only what the program says, so it goes wrong | The robot guesses the missing step correctly | The robot always repeats the first step | The robot ignores the whole program
test: Which of these is an invention built on the telephone and the radio? | The mobile phone | The wheel | The printing press | The candle
test: how's my msg get to my nan in canada so fast | It is split into packets that travel through cables, fibres and radio waves | It flies there by satellite pigeon | It goes by post overnight | It is printed and sent by plane
test: where's a website actually kept | On a computer called a server | Inside your own screen at home | In the air somewhere above the house | Inside the battery of your phone
test: my mate says it's fine to post your home address online cos you can delete it. true? | No, others can copy and keep it, so keep personal details private | Yes, deleting removes every copy | Yes, nobody can see posts | No, but you can post your phone number
test: a video said computers understand words just like people. right? | No, they store everything as numbers of 0s and 1s and follow programs | Yes, they think in English | Yes, they read like people | No, they understand only pictures
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a program? | A list of instructions which a computer follows | A machine which joins computers together across the world | A cable conveying flashes of light | A computer upon which websites are stored
exam: Ascertain the form in which a computer stores letters, pictures and sounds. | As numbers composed of 0s and 1s | As small printed images upon paper | As letters of the ordinary alphabet | As sounds recorded upon a tape
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the invention of the printing press. | Books became cheaper and knowledge spread more widely | Books became more costly and rarer than they were before | Handwriting was prohibited by law | The internet was established forthwith
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the transmission of a message across the internet. | It is divided into small packets, each bearing an address | It is conveyed whole as a single sealed letter | It is transmitted solely by telephone operators | It is carried exclusively by the postal service
